The model/ folder contains saved model objects used for future patient churn prediction. 
The main file is an .rds file, which is an R serialized object. In this project, 
the .rds file stores the trained Logistic Regression workflow, including both the preprocessing recipe and the fitted model. 
Saving the full workflow is important because it ensures that future patient datasets receive the same transformations, 
such as dummy encoding and normalization, before predictions are made.

The new dataset used for prediction must contain the same feature names and compatible data types as the training dataset. 
If required columns are missing or renamed, the saved workflow may fail or produce unreliable predictions.


```{r save-final-model, echo=TRUE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
# Fit final logistic regression workflow
final_lr_workflow <- workflow() %>%
  add_recipe(churn_recipe) %>%
  add_model(lr_spec) %>%
  fit(data = data_train)

# Save final model workflow
saveRDS(final_lr_workflow, "model/final_churn_logistic_workflow.rds")

# Save selected threshold
selected_threshold <- 0.30
saveRDS(selected_threshold, "model/selected_threshold.rds")
```


To load the model, include this rmd code and use the saved model

```{r load-and-use-model, echo=TRUE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
# Load saved model and threshold
final_model <- readRDS("model/final_churn_logistic_workflow.rds")
selected_threshold <- readRDS("model/selected_threshold.rds")

# New patient data must contain the same predictors used during training
# Example:
# new_data <- read.csv("data/new_patient_data.csv")

# Generate predicted probabilities
new_probabilities <- predict(final_model, new_data, type = "prob")

# Apply selected threshold
new_predictions <- new_probabilities %>%
  bind_cols(new_data) %>%
  mutate(
    churn_prediction = ifelse(.pred_Yes >= selected_threshold, "Churned", "Retained")
  )

# View results
new_predictions
```
